Great question! The Moon doesn’t make its own light. It’s like a big rocky mirror in space. Sunlight hits the Moon, and some of that light bounces off the Moon and travels to your eyes.

At night, your side of the Earth is turned away from the Sun, so the sky is dark. If the Moon is above you in the sky then, you can see its sunlit side easily. That’s why it looks bright and silvery.

The Moon isn’t only out at night, though. Sometimes it’s in the daytime sky too! But during the day the sky is so bright that it can be harder to see.

So: **Sun shines on the Moon → Moon reflects sunlight → your night sky is dark → you can see the Moon.** It doesn’t glow by itself.
